course: dictionary
lesson: 13322
title: Groynes on the coast
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Waves are always moving sand and pebbles along the coast. This is called longshore drift. A groyne is a low wall built out from the beach into the sea to slow this down. It helps keep the sand and pebbles on the beach. «The council built wooden groynes along the beach to stop the sand washing away.» Wooden, stone and concrete groynes all do the same job.
explain: Groynes are a kind of hard engineering, because people build them to change the coast. Look at the words around groyne in a sentence to find its meaning. «Because of the groynes, there was much more sand on one side of the beach.» The sand piles up on one side of each groyne, so the beach there is wider.
explain: Try this check. Swap in your meaning for the word. «The council built low walls out from the beach to stop the sand washing away.» Does it still make sense? If it does, you have understood the word.
fact: A groyne is a low wall or fence built out from a beach into the sea.
fact: Groynes can be made of wood, stone or concrete.
fact: Groynes stop sand and pebbles from being washed away along the coast.
fact: Longshore drift is the way waves move sand along the coast.
fact: In US spelling, groyne is spelled groin, but that is also a word for a part of the body, so check the context.
exercise: «The council built wooden groynes along the beach to stop the sand washing away.» What is a groyne? | A kind of seaweed that grows on rocks | A fish that lives in the sand | *A low wall built out from a beach into the sea | A tall lighthouse on a cliff
exercise: «Because of the groynes, there was much more sand on one side of the beach.» Why was there more sand on one side? | Children carried the sand there in buckets | The sea froze in winter | The groynes were painted bright red | *Waves moved sand along the coast and the groyne stopped it
exercise: What does longshore drift mean? | Sand blowing up into sand dunes | Water rushing down a river to the sea | *The way waves move sand and pebbles along the coast | A drink made at the seaside
test: Dan counted seven groynes «stretching out into the sea». Which way do groynes go? | *Out from the beach into the sea | Along the top of a cliff | Up into the sky | Under the sand
test: Which material is NOT listed for making groynes? | Wood | Stone | *Glass | Concrete
exam: Why do beaches with groynes often have more sand on one side? | Because the sea is salty | *Because longshore drift moves sand along the coast and the groyne stops it moving on | Because children dig there every day | Because groynes make new sand grow
